Q: Error using Simple Java Mail on a JavaFX project A: <p>For the older version you used at the time of writing you could have probably fixed it by including the underlying module like so, as Simple Java Mail pre 9.x releases didn't describe the transitive modules dependencies properly:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>module com.mycompany.proyectodam {
    requires javafx.controls;
    requires javafx.fxml;

    requires org.simplejavamail;
    requires jakarta.mail;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This was a known bug, see <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/issues/265" rel="nofollow noreferrer">simple-java-mail/issues/265</a>. This was implemented finally in the <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/master/RELEASE_HISTORY.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer">9.0.0 release</a>, java modules module descriptors are now properly defined and you don't need to work around this problem anymore.</p>

Q: Attach value to Chrome window that persists on restart A: <p>Bing helped me google this for you:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>. <strong>Use <code>chrome.storage</code> with a ID</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>Store the value in <code>chrome.storage.local</code> or <code>chrome.storage.session</code> (for session‑scoped data).</p>
</li>
<li><p>Example:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>async function saveWindowData(key, value) {
  await chrome.storage.local.set({
    [key]: value
  });
}

async function getWindowData(key) {
  const data = await chrome.storage.local.get(key);
  return data[key];
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<p>I would suggest not using a browser version dependent method, but instead using HTML5 storage.</p>
<p>Check that out here: <a href="https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webstorage.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.w3schools.com/html/html5_webstorage.asp</a></p>
